# tools/crypto_eth_address_witness.rish — the Ethereum address and its EIP-55 mixed-case checksum, authored in pure Rye as a composition over
# the GREEN Keccak-256 in crypto/keccak256.rye. An account address is the low twenty bytes of the Keccak-256 of an uncompressed public key; the
# human-facing form is EIP-55, the same forty hex characters cased by a bit of the Keccak-256 of the lowercase address string, so a corrupted
# address almost always fails the case pattern before a value is sent to a wrong destination (crypto · Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: crypto — Season G (the Six-Season double-seat). This rung authors NO new cryptography: it composes one already-GREEN stone — the
# Keccak-256 proven in crypto/keccak256.rye against the canonical known-answers and Zig's independent std.crypto — into the address derivation
# and the EIP-55 checksum, the natural next rung crypto/keccak256.rye's own note named. It is the Ethereum sibling of encoding/base58check.rye.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G · the crypto parity wave).
# Clean-room: the address rule and the EIP-55 algorithm are Ethereum's own public standard; the composition is ours, standing on our own
# parity-GREEN Keccak-256, never a copied line (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/eth_address.rye derives the twenty-byte address from the sixty-four-byte public key (Keccak-256, low twenty bytes) and renders EIP-55
# mixed case. Parity is proven at selftest against the four canonical EIP-55 reference addresses from the standard AND the end-to-end Mastering
# Ethereum worked example (public key 6e145c…32ccd0 → address 001d3f…ba0f9 → 0x001d3F1ef827552Ae1114027BD3ECF1f086bA0F9), plus the guards: a
# short public key refuses BadPubkeyLength, a wrong-length address BadAddressLength, a too-small destination OutTooSmall. Purely local — no key,
# no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_eth_address_witness.rish

say "crypto-eth-address: Language EN — an Ethereum address is the low twenty bytes of the Keccak-256 of an uncompressed public key. Its human-facing form is EIP-55: the same forty hex characters, but each letter upper- or lower-cased by a bit of the Keccak-256 of the lowercase address string. That casing is a checksum — a single mistyped or corrupted character almost always breaks the case pattern before a value is ever sent to a wrong destination. This module composes one already-GREEN rung and authors none of its own arithmetic."
say "crypto-eth-address: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: address_from_pubkey keeps the low twenty bytes of the Keccak-256 of the public key; to_checksummed renders those bytes as forty lowercase hex characters, hashes that string with the same Keccak-256, and uppercases every hex letter whose corresponding hash nibble is eight or greater — so the same address always renders the same way and a corrupted address is refused by the eye rather than trusted."
say "crypto-eth-address: Lens TAME — no new cryptography: every hash is the GREEN crypto/keccak256.rye, the permutation already proven on metal against the canonical known-answers and Zig's std.crypto. Fixed shapes (pubkey 64, address 20, checksummed 42), contract asserts on every function, destination length checked before the first write. Parity holds against the four canonical EIP-55 reference addresses AND the end-to-end Mastering Ethereum worked example; a short public key refuses BadPubkeyLength, a wrong-length address BadAddressLength, a too-small destination OutTooSmall — each before a partial result escapes. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/eth_address"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/eth_address.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-eth-address: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/eth_address.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-eth-address: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-eth-address: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-eth-address' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-eth-address: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-eth-address line"
